Title: gaze approves PRs it cannot have reviewed: no upper bound on the battery, no ticket-count check
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-09-10T10:17Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

`/gaze` classifies a PR into a battery tier from `(lines, files)` and stops
there. The ladder's top rung is `full` at 150 lines / 5 files, and Agent C's
panel maxes at five perspectives (`substantial → all five`). Above that rung
nothing changes: a 200-line PR and a 145 000-line PR get the same battery.

Measured over 30 days, 74 gaze invocations, joining transcript notifications
(which name every perspective agent that ran) to PR size:

- Depth does track size on average — Spearman 0.73 between lines and
  perspectives, medians 2 / 2 / 4 for tiny / small / full.
- But **60% of the corpus sits above the top rung**, where the tier stops
  discriminating.
- swfz#409: +142 753/-2 117 over **421 files**, four tickets bundled in one
  PR → 4 perspectives, APPROVED.
- swfz#3: +28 463/-542 over 46 files, six tickets closed at once → 3
  perspectives, APPROVED.

gaze has no lower bound on review-per-line. It therefore issues APPROVED on
PRs it cannot structurally have reviewed, and the verdict carries the same
weight as one on a 40-line diff. That is a merge criterion, which is what puts
this above the severity floor: the other findings from the same measurement
degrade quality, this one manufactures unfounded approval.

The harness already has the rule — `rules/workflow.md` § Autonomous action,
"Monster ticket → propose a decomposition", 15+ files. It exists at the
*ticket* level and gaze has never applied it at the *PR* level.

The ticket-count half is nearly free: gaze already parses the `Ticket:` line of
the PR body to know what to close. It never counts. `rules/git.md` § Merging
states "One ticket per PR is the review hygiene" — written, never checked. Both
monsters above announce multiple tickets in their own body, so the cheap check
catches the expensive case.

## Follow-on

0902 (`Blocked-by: 0901`) fixes the other half of the same measurement: the
tier reads size and never reads which paths the diff touches, so a session
hook got less review than a seven-line test. It waits on this ticket for a
measurement reason, not a code one — while 400-file PRs are still accepted,
they dominate any before/after comparison of a risk-routing change.

## Relevant files

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — phase 1 computes `pr_lines`/`pr_files` and the tier;
  § Circuit breakers holds the existing bail-out list; § Branch on verdict has
  the ESCALATE path this reuses.
- `tests/test_verify_fork_contracts.py` — where gaze's tier and telemetry
  contracts are already ratcheted; the new breaker belongs in the same file.
- `rules/workflow.md` § Autonomous action — the 15-file monster threshold to
  reuse rather than invent.
- `rules/git.md` § Merging — the one-ticket-per-PR hygiene the count check
  enforces.

## Actions

1. Phase 1, after the tier is computed: if the diff exceeds the un-reviewable
   threshold, ESCALATE with reason `un-reviewable: <n> files / <n> lines —
   split before review`, and do not spawn the battery. Reuse the 15-file
   threshold from `rules/workflow.md` rather than inventing a number; state in
   the skill text that it is borrowed, so the two move together.
2. Phase 1, same place: count the ticket close-claims in the PR body. More than
   one is not an error — `git.md` allows it when they genuinely land together —
   so emit a non-blocking `multi_ticket` finding the gate dispositions, not a
   refusal. Two or more claims *plus* the size breaker firing is the
   decomposition signal worth naming in the ESCALATE reason.
3. Record both in the § Circuit breakers list and in the § Output shape
   template, so the verdict comment says which breaker fired.

## Test

Red first, in `tests/test_verify_fork_contracts.py`:
`test_gaze_refuses_unreviewable_prs` — assert the skill body names an
un-reviewable circuit breaker keyed on file count, and that its threshold cites
`rules/workflow.md` rather than a bare literal. It must fail against the
current skill text; prove that by extracting the section from `origin/main`
before writing the fix, the way PR #860's ratchet was proved.

## Verification

- [ ] The breaker's threshold and `rules/workflow.md`'s monster threshold are
      the same number, and the skill says so.
- [ ] ESCALATE is reached without spawning phases 2-5 — the point is to not pay
      for a review that cannot be adequate.
- [ ] The multi-ticket check does NOT block on its own; a legitimately bundled
      pair still merges.
- [ ] `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- The gate (phase 6) stays invariant at every tier — this adds a pre-gate
  refusal, it must not weaken the gate.
- No change to the tiny/small/full thresholds; this ticket adds a rung above
  `full`, it does not move the existing ones.
- `--force-approve` still overrides, loudly, as it does for every other breaker.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A PR above the threshold returns ESCALATE naming the breaker, with no
      reviewer battery spawned.
- [ ] The verdict comment names which breaker fired.
- [ ] A multi-ticket PR below the threshold still gets a normal review, with a
      non-blocking finding recorded.
- [ ] Ratchet test present and proved red against the pre-fix text.
